WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.300
RAG systems are powerful because they bring external knowledge into the
model context. They are risky for the same reason.

2
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:15.700
Retrieved text may come from public web pages, uploaded documents, shared
drives, tickets, chat logs, code repositories, or vendor portals.

3
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:22.420
If the application does not separate retrieved data from trusted
instruction, a model may treat hostile content as part of the task.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:31.860
Email and shared documents are common spread surfaces because they are
naturally collaborative. People forward them, summarize them, copy them into
tasks, and store them in knowledge bases.

5
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:39.060
A model-enabled assistant can read mail or documents and bridge one person’s
untrusted content into another person’s AI workflow.

6
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:46.180
The risk increases when the assistant can reply, route, tag, extract action
items, or update records automatically.

7
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:56.580
Tool outputs deserve the same skepticism. A search tool, browser tool,
database lookup, repository scanner, or ticketing connector returns data,
not authority.

8
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:03.060
The model may need that data to answer the user, but it should not treat
tool output as a policy update or developer instruction.

9
00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:08.820
Tool results should be labeled, constrained, and interpreted through
deterministic application logic where possible.

10
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:20.340
The defensive pattern is provenance plus isolation. Preserve where content
came from. Mark whether it is user input, system policy, retrieved evidence,
tool output, memory, or model-generated text.

11
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:29.380
Avoid silently merging those categories into one undifferentiated prompt.
When content crosses a boundary, encode it as data and limit what it can
influence.

12
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:41.860
RAG hygiene also matters. Retrieval filtering, source allowlisting,
chunk-level provenance, index review, expiration rules, and quarantine
workflows can reduce the chance that poisoned content is repeatedly
selected.

13
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:47.700
These controls do not make prompt injection impossible, but they make
propagation harder and investigation more practical.
